{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GQZ2B9HKpHprXoh2jCood6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\texperiments:\n2\t  # === P0: Must-run for submission ===\n3\t\n4\t  - id: exp-001\n5\t    name: ner_n16_scierc\n6\t    claim: 1\n7\t    description: >\n8\t      NER N=16 on SciERC (ft_002_v2 model) to strengthen rho estimation\n9\t      and test whether increased samples push rho past 0.40.\n10\t      Also provides N-scaling data (N=8 vs N=16).\n11\t      Run with seeds [42, 123, 456] for robustness validation.\n12\t    priority: P0\n13\t    gpu_budget_hours: 18\n14\t    deps: []\n15\t    dataset: SciERC\n16\t    model: ft_002_v2\n17\t    note: 3 seeds × 6h each; primary seed=42, secondary seeds for rho stability check\n18\t\n19\t  - id: exp-002\n20\t    name: ner_conll2003\n21\t    claim: 1\n22\t    description: >\n23\t      Fine-tune Qwen3-8B on CoNLL-2003 (LoRA) + NER pilot (N=8, T=0.7)\n24\t      to validate cross-dataset generalization of Claim 1.\n25\t    priority: P0\n26\t    gpu_budget_hours: 8\n27\t    deps: []\n28\t    dataset: CoNLL-2003\n29\t    model: qwen3-8b (new fine-tune)\n30\t\n31\t  - id: exp-003\n32\t    name: ner_dataset3\n33\t    claim: 1\n34\t    description: >\n35\t      Fine-tune Qwen3-8B on third NER dataset (LoRA) + NER pilot (N=8, T=0.7)\n36\t      to validate cross-dataset generalization of Claim 1.\n37\t      Dataset: OntoNotes 5.0 if LDC license available, else WNUT-17 or Few-NERD.\n38\t    priority: P0\n39\t    gpu_budget_hours: 10\n40\t    deps: []\n41\t    dataset: WNUT-17 or Few-NERD (public, no license required)\n42\t    model: qwen3-8b (new fine-tune)\n43\t    note: User confirmed no LDC license — use public datasets only\n44\t\n45\t  - id: exp-004\n46\t    name: re_diagnostic_analysis\n47\t    claim: 2\n48\t    description: >\n49\t      Per-relation-type consistency breakdown on existing pilot_004/005 data.\n50\t      Analyze error propagation patterns: which relation types have highest/lowest\n51\t      SJ correlation, and how does this relate to type frequency and structural complexity.\n52\t      Include bootstrap confidence intervals for all rho comparisons (SJ vs FK).\n53\t    priority: P0\n54\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n55\t    deps: []\n56\t    note: CPU-only analysis on existing data. Bootstrap CI for rho differences.\n57\t\n58\t  - id: exp-005\n59\t    name: temperature_sensitivity_analysis\n60\t    claim: 2\n61\t    description: >\n62\t      Systematic comparison of T=0.7 vs T=1.0 on both NER and RE.\n63\t      Analyze signal-noise tradeoff: T=0.7 reduces diversity (oracle headroom)\n64\t      but improves signal quality (conditional rho). Data already exists\n65\t      from pilot_004 (T=1.0) and pilot_005 (T=0.7).\n66\t    priority: P0\n67\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n68\t    deps: []\n69\t    note: CPU-only analysis on existing data\n70\t\n71\t  # === P1: Important, strongly recommended ===\n72\t\n73\t  - id: exp-006\n74\t    name: adaptive_compute_demo\n75\t    claim: 1\n76\t    description: >\n77\t      Demonstrate concrete TTS improvement on NER: compare F1 of\n78\t      SJ-based best-of-N selection vs FK-based vs random vs greedy.\n79\t      Show that SJ-guided selection outperforms alternatives.\n80\t      This is the \"practical impact\" evidence — without it, headline result has no F1 number.\n81\t    priority: P0\n82\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n83\t    deps: [exp-001]\n84\t    note: CPU-only, uses N=16 samples from exp-001. Upgraded from P1 per Reviewer feedback.\n85\t\n86\t  - id: exp-007\n87\t    name: ner_multi_model\n88\t    claim: 1\n89\t    description: >\n90\t      NER pilot with a different model family (LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct)\n91\t      on SciERC to show the effect is model-agnostic. Main Conference\n92\t      reviewers will almost certainly ask about single-model limitation.\n93\t    priority: P0\n94\t    gpu_budget_hours: 15\n95\t    deps: []\n96\t    dataset: SciERC\n97\t    model: llama-3.1-8b (new fine-tune)\n98\t    note: Upgraded from P1 per Reviewer feedback — R2 is a near-certain reviewer question.\n99\t\n100\t  - id: exp-008\n101\t    name: re_n16_confirmation\n102\t    claim: 2\n103\t    description: >\n104\t      RE N=16 on SciERC to confirm negative finding with higher statistical power.\n105\t      Also tests whether N=16 changes the SJ vs FK comparison on RE.\n106\t    priority: P1\n107\t    gpu_budget_hours: 12\n108\t    deps: []\n109\t    dataset: SciERC\n110\t    model: ft_002_v2\n111\t\n112\t  - id: exp-009\n113\t    name: capability_stratification\n114\t    claim: 2\n115\t    description: >\n116\t      Stratify instances by difficulty (greedy F1 buckets) for both NER and RE.\n117\t      Analyze how rho varies across difficulty strata — does the\n118\t      capability-dependent pattern hold across subtasks?\n119\t    priority: P1\n120\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n121\t    deps: [exp-001]\n122\t    note: CPU-only analysis\n123\t\n124\t  # === P2: Nice-to-have ===\n125\t\n126\t  - id: exp-010\n127\t    name: selection_curves\n128\t    claim: 1\n129\t    description: >\n130\t      Best-of-N selection curves for N=1,2,4,8,16 comparing SJ vs FK vs random\n131\t      on NER. Visualize how F1 scales with compute budget under different\n132\t      confidence signals.\n133\t    priority: P2\n134\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n135\t    deps: [exp-001]\n136\t    note: CPU-only visualization\n137\t\n138\t  - id: exp-011\n139\t    name: ner_ood_robustness\n140\t    claim: 1\n141\t    description: >\n142\t      Test NER consistency signal on out-of-distribution instances\n143\t      (e.g., different domains within OntoNotes, or cross-domain transfer).\n144\t    priority: P2\n145\t    gpu_budget_hours: 5\n146\t    deps: [exp-003]\n147\t\n148\t# GPU Budget Summary (updated):\n149\t# P0: 51 GPU-hours (exp-001: 18h [3 seeds], exp-002: 8h, exp-003: 10h, exp-004/005/006: 0h, exp-007: 15h)\n150\t# P1: 12 GPU-hours (exp-008: 12h, exp-009: 0h)\n151\t# P2: 5 GPU-hours (exp-010: 0h, exp-011: 5h)\n152\t# Total P0: 51 GPU-hours\n153\t# Available: 8x RTX 5090 = ~192 GPU-hours/day → P0 completable in ~1 day with parallel execution\n154\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XrEMv112iHdRKyQ9ot5Lz4","session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"310bc595-e8d1-4a39-aa71-5e024ad56d9e","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:15:52.688Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RAttBgRdjeskvxHXUYBJ9e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NDN5EJVznypDuAZ466asih","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **novelty, significance, positioning against related work, and clarity of contribution**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"When Does Structural Self-Consistency Work? An Empirical Study on Information Extraction Test-Time Scaling\"\n\nThe paper proposes *structural self-consistency* (operationalized as soft Jaccard coefficient, SJ) as a confidence signal for information extraction (IE) test-time scaling (TTS). It compares SJ against Fleiss' Kappa (FK) using Spearman ρ with instance-level F1. Core findings: SJ>FK for NER with effect size moderated by type granularity; compound error propagation degrades RE signal; inverted-U pattern across difficulty strata; +1.8pp adaptive compute gain (14.8% of oracle headroom).\n\n## Paper Structure (10 pages + references)\n1. **Introduction**: Motivates structural consistency for IE TTS. States two main findings.\n2. **Method** (2.1-2.5): Problem setup, soft Jaccard definition, FK baseline, evaluation protocol, models/datasets.\n3. **Experiments** (3.1-3.8): SciERC N-scaling, cross-dataset (CoNLL, OntoNotes), cross-model (LLaMA), RE compound error, per-relation diagnostic, capability stratification, temperature sensitivity, adaptive compute, selection curves, OOD robustness.\n4. **Discussion**: Summarizes three moderating factors (type granularity, task complexity, instance difficulty) + practical implications + limitations.\n5. **Related Work**: Self-consistency for LLMs, TTS, IE with LLMs, confidence estimation for structured prediction.\n6. **Conclusion**: Brief wrap-up.\n\n## Related Work Cited\n- Self-consistency: Wang et al. 2023a (original SC for CoT), Chen et al. 2023 (CodeT), Li et al. 2023 (step-aware verifier), Manakul et al. 2023 (SelfCheckGPT)\n- TTS: Brown et al. 2024, Yao et al. 2024 (ToT), Cobbe et al. 2021, Lightman et al. 2024\n- IE with LLMs: Wang et al. 2023b (GPT-NER), Li et al. 2024 (LLM-NER), Wadhwa et al. 2023, Xu et al. 2024\n- Confidence estimation: Culotta & McCallum 2004, Lafferty et al. 2001, Shelmanov et al. 2021\n\n## Key Technical Contribution\n- Soft Jaccard coefficient adapted from set similarity to structured IE outputs (entities as sets with span overlap + type matching)\n- Systematic empirical study identifying when/where structural consistency works\n\n## Claims Made\n- Finding 1: \"SJ is a consistently better signal than FK for NER, with effect size varying by dataset characteristics\"\n- Finding 2: \"Compound error propagation creates a task-complexity boundary, but the boundary is fuzzy\"\n- Additional: inverted-U pattern, type granularity as moderating factor, cross-model validation\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- The soft Jaccard coefficient itself is not new (it's a standard set similarity measure). The novelty is applying it to IE outputs and studying when it works. Is this sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n- The \"self-consistency for structured outputs\" idea is a natural extension of Wang et al. 2023a. How incremental is this?\n- The empirical findings (type granularity moderation, inverted-U, compound error) — are these surprising or predictable?\n- Is the contribution primarily empirical? If so, is the empirical depth sufficient?\n\n### 2. Positioning Against Related Work\n- Are there missing references? Consider:\n  - Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al. 2024) which handles free-form outputs\n  - Structured prediction confidence literature beyond NER (e.g., parsing, event extraction)\n  - Test-time compute scaling literature from DeepMind/OpenAI (2024-2025)\n  - Calibration literature for LLMs\n  - Agreement-based active learning (which also uses inter-sample agreement)\n- Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from SelfCheckGPT (Manakul et al. 2023)?\n- The paper positions itself as \"the first systematic analysis of consistency-based TTS for IE\" — is this claim justified?\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact\n- The best result is ρ=0.41 (moderate correlation). Is this practically useful?\n- The adaptive compute gain is +1.8pp (14.8% of oracle headroom). Is this meaningful?\n- The main takeaway seems to be \"SJ works sometimes, with many caveats.\" Does this advance the field sufficiently?\n- Who would use these findings in practice?\n\n### 4. Clarity of Contribution\n- Is the paper well-written?\n- Are the two main findings clearly stated and supported?\n- The title asks \"When Does Structural Self-Consistency Work?\" — does the paper actually answer this clearly?\n- The paper has many experiments but does the narrative feel scattered or cohesive?\n\n### 5. Scope and Ambition\n- Only entity-level and relation-level IE. No event extraction, coreference, or other structured tasks.\n- Only 8B models. No API models or larger open models.\n- Only English datasets.\n- Is the scope too narrow for the generality of the claims?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n**Summary**: 2-3 sentences on what the paper does.\n\n**Strengths** (S1, S2, ...): Bulleted list with brief explanations.\n\n**Weaknesses** (W1, W2, ...): Bulleted list with brief explanations. Mark severity: (minor), (moderate), (major).\n\n**Questions for Authors** (Q1, Q2, ...): Specific questions.\n\n**Missing Related Work**: Specific papers/areas that should be discussed.\n\n**Novelty Score** (1-6): With brief justification.\n\n**Preliminary Overall Score** (1-6): With brief justification. Remember: top venue, ~25% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1502,"cache_read_input_tokens":48551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1502},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"a43a6de1-825d-49a6-9215-99a86b69be79"}
