{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QKv8jeAsbEtt8yEc2pcMRQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{caption}\n14\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\rhosj}{\\rho_{\\mathrm{SJ}}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\rhofk}{\\rho_{\\mathrm{FK}}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\SJ}{\\mathrm{SJ}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\FK}{\\mathrm{FK}}\n20\t\n21\t\\title{Structural Self-Consistency Enables Test-Time Scaling\\\\for Entity-Level Information Extraction}\n22\t\n23\t\\author{Anonymous}\n24\t\n25\t\\date{}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t%=====================================================================\n31\t\\begin{abstract}\n32\tTest-time scaling (TTS) through sampling and selection requires a reliable confidence signal to choose among candidate outputs. While self-consistency has proven effective for reasoning tasks via majority voting, structured prediction tasks such as information extraction (IE) pose a distinct challenge: outputs have internal structure (entity spans, types, relations) that surface-level agreement metrics fail to capture. We introduce \\emph{structural self-consistency}, which uses soft Jaccard similarity over structured outputs as a confidence signal for IE test-time scaling.\n33\tAcross three NER benchmarks (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes), structural consistency (soft Jaccard) achieves Spearman $\\rho\\!=\\!0.40$--$0.45$ correlation with instance-level F1, significantly outperforming Fleiss' Kappa ($\\rho\\!=\\!0.29$--$0.32$, $p\\!<\\!10^{-27}$). This translates to a +3.9 F1 improvement on SciERC NER through best-of-$N$ selection at $N\\!=\\!16$ with zero additional training. However, on relation extraction, full-set correlation drops to $\\rho\\!=\\!0.27$ due to compound error propagation across head entity, tail entity, and relation type components. Conditional analysis---excluding instances where the model lacks basic capability---recovers $\\rho\\!=\\!0.40$ with soft Jaccard outperforming Fleiss' Kappa by 6.3 points, establishing a principled boundary condition for when structural consistency works as a TTS signal. Our findings are robust across two model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), out-of-distribution settings, and sample budgets from $N\\!=\\!2$ to $N\\!=\\!16$.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t%=====================================================================\n37\t\\section{Introduction}\n38\t\n39\tTest-time scaling (TTS) has emerged as a practical strategy for improving language model performance without additional training: generate multiple candidate outputs, then select the best one using a confidence signal. For reasoning tasks, self-consistency---the observation that correct answers tend to recur across samples---enables effective majority voting. However, structured prediction tasks such as information extraction (IE) produce complex outputs (sets of entity mentions, relation triplets) where the notion of ``agreement'' is non-trivial.\n40\t\n41\tConsider named entity recognition (NER): two sampled outputs may predict overlapping but non-identical entity sets. A surface-level metric like Fleiss' Kappa treats each token label independently, ignoring that two predictions sharing 4 of 5 entity spans are far more similar than two sharing 0 of 5. This motivates \\emph{structural} similarity metrics that respect the set-based nature of IE outputs.\n42\t\n43\tWe study whether structural self-consistency---measured by soft Jaccard similarity over predicted entity/relation sets---provides a reliable confidence signal for IE test-time scaling. Our investigation yields two main findings:\n44\t\n45\t\\textbf{Claim 1 (Positive):} For entity-level IE, structural self-consistency significantly outperforms surface agreement as a confidence signal, enabling effective test-time scaling. Across three NER datasets spanning different domains (scientific text, news, mixed genres) and entity type granularities (4--18 types), soft Jaccard achieves $\\rho\\!=\\!0.40$--$0.45$ correlation with accuracy, consistently surpassing Fleiss' Kappa by 8--15 points. This correlation translates to concrete F1 gains through best-of-$N$ selection.\n46\t\n47\t\\textbf{Claim 2 (Diagnostic):} The effectiveness of consistency-based confidence is governed by task structural complexity. Compound error propagation in relational tasks degrades the consistency-accuracy correlation on the full instance set, but the signal remains valid within the model's capability range. Per-relation-type analysis reveals that high-frequency, short-distance relations achieve NER-level consistency, while low-frequency, long-distance relations degrade to near-chance. This establishes a principled boundary condition: structural consistency measures \\emph{uncertainty}, not \\emph{inability}, and its diagnostic value depends on the prevalence of knowledge-gap instances.\n48\t\n49\tOur contributions are: (1) the first systematic study of structural self-consistency as a TTS confidence signal for IE; (2) empirical evidence across 3 datasets, 2 model families, and in-/out-of-distribution settings; (3) a diagnostic framework identifying when and why consistency-based TTS succeeds or fails in structured prediction.\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XrEMv112iHdRKyQ9ot5Lz4","session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"7a193809-1e55-46a5-91c6-dfeb89e473fb","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:16:11.105Z"}
