{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BevfFduHQydXHJZDGvibsU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t% microtype omitted for compatibility\n13\t\n14\t\\title{When Does Structural Self-Consistency Work?\\\\ An Empirical Study on Information Extraction Test-Time Scaling}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{\n17\t  Anonymous Authors\n18\t}\n19\t\n20\t\\date{}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%==============================================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tTest-time scaling (TTS) via sampling and selection requires a reliable confidence signal to choose among candidate outputs.\n28\tSelf-consistency---majority voting over sampled outputs---has proven effective for reasoning tasks, but structured prediction tasks like information extraction (IE) pose unique challenges: outputs have internal structure (entities, relations), and surface agreement metrics miss structural similarity.\n29\tWe propose \\emph{structural self-consistency}, operationalized as soft Jaccard similarity over structured outputs, as a confidence signal for IE test-time scaling.\n30\tThrough experiments on three NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes) and relation extraction on SciERC, we find that structural self-consistency consistently outperforms surface agreement (Fleiss' Kappa) as a predictor of output accuracy for NER ($\\rho{=}0.41$ vs $0.29$, $p{<}10^{-22}$ on SciERC), with the advantage replicating across datasets and model families (Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B).\n31\tHowever, the effect size varies with dataset properties: the SJ--FK gap narrows from $+$12pp on SciERC (6 entity types) to $+$3pp on OntoNotes (18 types), revealing entity type granularity as a key moderating factor.\n32\tFor relation extraction, compound error propagation fundamentally degrades the consistency--accuracy correlation on the full instance set ($\\rho{=}0.26$), though the signal remains valid within the model's capability range (conditional $\\rho{=}0.33$).\n33\tCapability stratification analysis reveals an inverted-U pattern: structural consistency is most informative for medium-difficulty instances, where the model exhibits genuine uncertainty rather than consistent success or failure.\n34\tSJ-based sample selection yields $+$1.8 F1 points over greedy decoding on SciERC NER, capturing 14.8\\% of oracle headroom.\n35\tOur findings provide empirical guidelines for when structural self-consistency is an effective TTS signal for structured prediction.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%==============================================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\n41\tTest-time scaling (TTS)---allocating additional computation at inference time to improve output quality---has emerged as a powerful paradigm for large language models (LLMs).\n42\tA common TTS strategy samples multiple outputs and selects the best one using a confidence signal.\n43\tFor reasoning tasks, self-consistency \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency} uses majority voting: if many sampled chain-of-thought paths arrive at the same answer, that answer is likely correct.\n44\tThis approach succeeds because reasoning outputs are atomic (a single number or label) and exact-match agreement is meaningful.\n45\t\n46\tStructured prediction tasks such as information extraction (IE) present a fundamentally different challenge.\n47\tIE outputs---sets of entities with types and spans, or relational triplets---have internal structure that surface-level agreement metrics fail to capture.\n48\tTwo NER outputs may share most entities but disagree on a single boundary or type, yielding low exact-match agreement despite high structural similarity.\n49\tConversely, two outputs may happen to agree on a few entities while differing in overall structure.\n50\tThis mismatch motivates a \\emph{structure-aware} consistency metric.\n51\t\n52\tWe introduce \\textbf{structural self-consistency} for IE, operationalized as the soft Jaccard coefficient computed over structured outputs.\n53\tGiven $N$ stochastic samples from an LLM, we compute pairwise soft Jaccard similarity and average over all pairs to obtain an instance-level consistency score.\n54\tOur central question is: \\emph{does this structural consistency score correlate with output accuracy, and if so, under what conditions?}\n55\t\n56\tOur investigation yields two main findings:\n57\t\n58\t\\paragraph{Finding 1: SJ is a consistently better confidence signal than FK for NER, with effect size varying by dataset characteristics.}\n59\tAcross three NER datasets, structural self-consistency (SJ) yields higher Spearman correlation with accuracy than Fleiss' Kappa (FK).\n60\tThe advantage is largest on SciERC ($+$12pp, 6 entity types), moderate on CoNLL-2003 ($+$7pp, 4 types), and smallest on OntoNotes ($+$3pp, 18 types).\n61\tThis gradient reveals that SJ's advantage is modulated by entity type granularity: when type confusion is the dominant error source (as in OntoNotes with 18 fine-grained types), structural overlap becomes a less reliable proxy for accuracy.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Finding 2: Compound error propagation creates a task-complexity boundary, but the boundary is fuzzy.}\n64\tOn relation extraction, where correct output requires jointly identifying head entity, tail entity, and relation type, full-set correlation drops to $\\rho{=}0.26$.\n65\tConditioning on instances where the model demonstrates non-zero capability recovers a modest signal ($\\rho{=}0.33$), but the conditional threshold is sensitive and the separation between capability regimes is not clean.\n66\t\n67\tThese findings provide practical guidance: structural self-consistency is most effective for entity-level tasks with moderate type granularity, on medium-difficulty instances within the model's capability frontier.\n68\tWe validate these conclusions across two model families (Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B) and demonstrate that SJ-based selection yields $+$1.8 F1 points over greedy decoding, though this captures only 14.8\\% of oracle headroom, indicating substantial room for improvement.\n69\t\n70\t%==============================================================================\n71\t\\section{Method}\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{Problem Setup}\n74\t\n75\tGiven a fine-tuned LLM $M$ and an input instance $x$, we generate $N$ stochastic outputs $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ by sampling from $M$ at temperature $T$.\n76\tEach output $y_i$ is a structured object: for NER, a set of (span, type) tuples; for RE, a set of (head, tail, relation) triplets.\n77\tOur goal is to compute a \\emph{consistency score} $c(x)$ from $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ that correlates with the accuracy of the best output, enabling confidence-based selection or abstention.\n78\t\n79\t\\subsection{Structural Self-Consistency via Soft Jaccard}\n80\t\n81\tWe define structural similarity between two outputs $y_i$ and $y_j$ using the \\textbf{soft Jaccard coefficient}.\n82\tFor NER, let $E_i$ and $E_j$ be the entity sets extracted from $y_i$ and $y_j$.\n83\tWe compute a soft match score between entities based on span overlap and type agreement:\n84\t\\begin{equation}\n85\t  \\text{match}(e_a, e_b) = \\text{IoU}(\\text{span}(e_a), \\text{span}(e_b)) \\cdot \\mathbf{1}[\\text{type}(e_a) = \\text{type}(e_b)]\n86\t\\end{equation}\n87\twhere IoU is the token-level intersection-over-union of spans.\n88\tThe soft Jaccard between two entity sets is:\n89\t\\begin{equation}\n90\t  \\text{SJ}(E_i, E_j) = \\frac{\\sum_{(a,b) \\in \\mathcal{M}^*} \\text{match}(e_a, e_b)}{\\max(|E_i|, |E_j|)}\n91\t\\end{equation}\n92\twhere $\\mathcal{M}^*$ is the optimal bipartite matching.\n93\tThe instance-level consistency score averages over all $\\binom{N}{2}$ pairs:\n94\t\\begin{equation}\n95\t  c_{\\text{SJ}}(x) = \\frac{2}{N(N{-}1)} \\sum_{i<j} \\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)\n96\t\\end{equation}\n97\t\n98\tFor relation extraction, the soft Jaccard extends to triplets by requiring soft matches on head span, tail span, and relation type jointly.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Baseline: Fleiss' Kappa}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NDN5EJVznypDuAZ466asih","session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"c9138716-0201-41bd-9dbc-352a94992158","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:16:35.200Z"}
