{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EPNNW4Q3gu51Tb94y5tAgw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016NY9Z3vMBc144mNm3bbfNr","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/worst/paper.tex","limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15110,"cache_read_input_tokens":56066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15110,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NDN5EJVznypDuAZ466asih","session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"42e7e853-edab-4d4e-baf6-351d71414aa4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016NY9Z3vMBc144mNm3bbfNr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage{times}\n3\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n4\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{url}\n12\t\n13\t\\title{Why Self-Consistency Fails as a Confidence Signal\\\\for Structured Prediction}\n14\t\n15\t\\author{Anonymous}\n16\t\n17\t\\begin{document}\n18\t\\maketitle\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{abstract}\n21\tSelf-consistency---selecting the most agreed-upon output among multiple samples---has emerged as a powerful test-time scaling strategy for LLM reasoning tasks.\n22\tA natural extension is to use consistency as a \\emph{confidence signal} for structured prediction, where outputs have rich internal structure (entities, relations, triplets).\n23\tWe systematically evaluate two consistency metrics---structural soft Jaccard (SJ) and surface-level Fleiss' Kappa (FK)---as confidence estimators for information extraction across three NER datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, WNUT-17), one RE dataset (SciERC), two model families (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), and sampling budgets $N \\in \\{2,4,8,16\\}$.\n24\tOur findings are predominantly negative.\n25\tWhile SJ achieves moderate correlation with accuracy on one dataset--model combination (SciERC NER with Qwen3-8B, $\\rho{=}0.38$), this advantage does not transfer across datasets ($\\rho{=}0.22$ on CoNLL-2003, $\\rho{=}0.19$ on WNUT-17), models (LLaMA-3.1-8B: SJ$<$FK), or tasks (RE: $\\rho{=}0.24$).\n26\tConsistency-based best-of-$N$ selection fails to outperform random selection ($p{=}0.47$), despite substantial oracle headroom (7.5 F1 points).\n27\tThrough error decomposition, capability stratification, and temperature sensitivity analysis, we identify three failure modes: a performance ceiling effect, a floor effect from capability gaps, and model-specific confounds in sampling behavior.\n28\tWe conclude with practitioner guidelines delineating the narrow conditions under which consistency signals may be informative.\n29\t\\end{abstract}\n30\t\n31\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n32\t\\section{Introduction}\n33\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n34\t\n35\tTest-time scaling (TTS) through repeated sampling and selection has become a prominent strategy for improving LLM performance~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency,brown2024large}.\n36\tThe core premise is simple: sample multiple candidate outputs, estimate confidence via inter-sample agreement, and select the most confident prediction.\n37\tFor reasoning tasks with discrete final answers, majority voting provides a natural and effective confidence signal~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency}.\n38\t\n39\tStructured prediction tasks---such as named entity recognition (NER) and relation extraction (RE)---pose a qualitatively different challenge.\n40\tOutputs are not single tokens but complex structures: sets of typed entity spans, or sets of (head, relation, tail) triplets.\n41\tTwo outputs may partially overlap in non-trivial ways, making simple majority voting inapplicable.\n42\tThis motivates \\emph{structural} consistency metrics that capture soft agreement between structured outputs.\n43\t\n44\tWe investigate whether structural self-consistency can serve as a reliable confidence signal for information extraction.\n45\tSpecifically, we compare soft Jaccard similarity (SJ), which measures set-level overlap between structured outputs, against Fleiss' Kappa (FK), which treats outputs as flat token sequences.\n46\tOur hypothesis---that SJ's structural awareness should yield a stronger confidence signal---is tested across a comprehensive experimental matrix spanning datasets, models, tasks, temperatures, and sampling budgets.\n47\t\n48\tThe results are sobering.\n49\tSJ achieves a meaningful correlation ($\\rho{=}0.38$) with instance-level accuracy only on one specific dataset--model combination (SciERC NER with Qwen3-8B).\n50\tThis finding does not generalize:"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NDN5EJVznypDuAZ466asih","session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"e5f745b7-cdc1-480f-9e3e-d4d83e9a0cbb","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:16:41.928Z"}
