{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NDN5EJVznypDuAZ466asih","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)** | **Novelty Score: 2/6**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper provides a thorough empirical study with honest treatment of negative results, but falls short of the top-venue bar on three critical dimensions:\n\n1. **Thin novelty** -- The contribution is metric substitution (soft Jaccard for Fleiss' Kappa) without theoretical justification. The \"structural\" framing overstates what is essentially set-level scoring. The implementation uses type-grouped Hungarian matching, which meaningfully differs from Equation 1's symmetric max-match formulation.\n\n2. **Missing critical baselines** -- Comparison is exclusively against Fleiss' Kappa, which is a weak strawman. Absent baselines include sequence log-probability (zero additional inference cost), Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024, the most directly relevant prior work), exact-match voting rate, and semantic uncertainty methods. Without these, we cannot determine if SJ's advantage reflects genuine merit or just FK's inadequacy.\n\n3. **Methodological issues** -- Inconsistent N across Table 1 (N=16 for SciERC vs N=8 for others), formula-implementation discrepancy, missing confidence intervals, and only 10 references in the bibliography.\n\n**Strongest elements:** The diagnostic analysis (inverted-U capability pattern, compound error decomposition for RE, temperature as capability filter) is genuinely insightful and represents the paper's best path to acceptance. The +3.9 F1 practical gain and cross-dataset/cross-model replication are solid.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** Add stronger baselines (especially log-prob and USC), fix the formula discrepancy, standardize experimental conditions in Table 1, expand references to 30+, and reframe as \"set-aware consistency scoring with diagnostic analysis.\"\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a1262c4c43a4c851a (use SendMessage with to: 'a1262c4c43a4c851a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 103738\ntool_uses: 29\nduration_ms: 181987</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"dee18fcb-d290-4f1c-9018-1b59fe225d70","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:18:57.771Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **novelty, significance, positioning against related work, and clarity of contribution**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"When Does Structural Self-Consistency Work? An Empirical Study on Information Extraction Test-Time Scaling\"\n\nThe paper proposes *structural self-consistency* (operationalized as soft Jaccard coefficient, SJ) as a confidence signal for information extraction (IE) test-time scaling (TTS). It compares SJ against Fleiss' Kappa (FK) using Spearman ρ with instance-level F1. Core findings: SJ>FK for NER with effect size moderated by type granularity; compound error propagation degrades RE signal; inverted-U pattern across difficulty strata; +1.8pp adaptive compute gain (14.8% of oracle headroom).\n\n## Paper Structure (10 pages + references)\n1. **Introduction**: Motivates structural consistency for IE TTS. States two main findings.\n2. **Method** (2.1-2.5): Problem setup, soft Jaccard definition, FK baseline, evaluation protocol, models/datasets.\n3. **Experiments** (3.1-3.8): SciERC N-scaling, cross-dataset (CoNLL, OntoNotes), cross-model (LLaMA), RE compound error, per-relation diagnostic, capability stratification, temperature sensitivity, adaptive compute, selection curves, OOD robustness.\n4. **Discussion**: Summarizes three moderating factors (type granularity, task complexity, instance difficulty) + practical implications + limitations.\n5. **Related Work**: Self-consistency for LLMs, TTS, IE with LLMs, confidence estimation for structured prediction.\n6. **Conclusion**: Brief wrap-up.\n\n## Related Work Cited\n- Self-consistency: Wang et al. 2023a (original SC for CoT), Chen et al. 2023 (CodeT), Li et al. 2023 (step-aware verifier), Manakul et al. 2023 (SelfCheckGPT)\n- TTS: Brown et al. 2024, Yao et al. 2024 (ToT), Cobbe et al. 2021, Lightman et al. 2024\n- IE with LLMs: Wang et al. 2023b (GPT-NER), Li et al. 2024 (LLM-NER), Wadhwa et al. 2023, Xu et al. 2024\n- Confidence estimation: Culotta & McCallum 2004, Lafferty et al. 2001, Shelmanov et al. 2021\n\n## Key Technical Contribution\n- Soft Jaccard coefficient adapted from set similarity to structured IE outputs (entities as sets with span overlap + type matching)\n- Systematic empirical study identifying when/where structural consistency works\n\n## Claims Made\n- Finding 1: \"SJ is a consistently better signal than FK for NER, with effect size varying by dataset characteristics\"\n- Finding 2: \"Compound error propagation creates a task-complexity boundary, but the boundary is fuzzy\"\n- Additional: inverted-U pattern, type granularity as moderating factor, cross-model validation\n\n## Your Review Tasks\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n- The soft Jaccard coefficient itself is not new (it's a standard set similarity measure). The novelty is applying it to IE outputs and studying when it works. Is this sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n- The \"self-consistency for structured outputs\" idea is a natural extension of Wang et al. 2023a. How incremental is this?\n- The empirical findings (type granularity moderation, inverted-U, compound error) — are these surprising or predictable?\n- Is the contribution primarily empirical? If so, is the empirical depth sufficient?\n\n### 2. Positioning Against Related Work\n- Are there missing references? Consider:\n  - Universal Self-Consistency (USC, Chen et al. 2024) which handles free-form outputs\n  - Structured prediction confidence literature beyond NER (e.g., parsing, event extraction)\n  - Test-time compute scaling literature from DeepMind/OpenAI (2024-2025)\n  - Calibration literature for LLMs\n  - Agreement-based active learning (which also uses inter-sample agreement)\n- Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from SelfCheckGPT (Manakul et al. 2023)?\n- The paper positions itself as \"the first systematic analysis of consistency-based TTS for IE\" — is this claim justified?\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact\n- The best result is ρ=0.41 (moderate correlation). Is this practically useful?\n- The adaptive compute gain is +1.8pp (14.8% of oracle headroom). Is this meaningful?\n- The main takeaway seems to be \"SJ works sometimes, with many caveats.\" Does this advance the field sufficiently?\n- Who would use these findings in practice?\n\n### 4. Clarity of Contribution\n- Is the paper well-written?\n- Are the two main findings clearly stated and supported?\n- The title asks \"When Does Structural Self-Consistency Work?\" — does the paper actually answer this clearly?\n- The paper has many experiments but does the narrative feel scattered or cohesive?\n\n### 5. Scope and Ambition\n- Only entity-level and relation-level IE. No event extraction, coreference, or other structured tasks.\n- Only 8B models. No API models or larger open models.\n- Only English datasets.\n- Is the scope too narrow for the generality of the claims?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n**Summary**: 2-3 sentences on what the paper does.\n\n**Strengths** (S1, S2, ...): Bulleted list with brief explanations.\n\n**Weaknesses** (W1, W2, ...): Bulleted list with brief explanations. Mark severity: (minor), (moderate), (major).\n\n**Questions for Authors** (Q1, Q2, ...): Specific questions.\n\n**Missing Related Work**: Specific papers/areas that should be discussed.\n\n**Novelty Score** (1-6): With brief justification.\n\n**Preliminary Overall Score** (1-6): With brief justification. Remember: top venue, ~25% acceptance rate.","agentId":"a1262c4c43a4c851a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)** | **Novelty Score: 2/6**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper provides a thorough empirical study with honest treatment of negative results, but falls short of the top-venue bar on three critical dimensions:\n\n1. **Thin novelty** -- The contribution is metric substitution (soft Jaccard for Fleiss' Kappa) without theoretical justification. The \"structural\" framing overstates what is essentially set-level scoring. The implementation uses type-grouped Hungarian matching, which meaningfully differs from Equation 1's symmetric max-match formulation.\n\n2. **Missing critical baselines** -- Comparison is exclusively against Fleiss' Kappa, which is a weak strawman. Absent baselines include sequence log-probability (zero additional inference cost), Universal Self-Consistency (Chen et al., NAACL 2024, the most directly relevant prior work), exact-match voting rate, and semantic uncertainty methods. Without these, we cannot determine if SJ's advantage reflects genuine merit or just FK's inadequacy.\n\n3. **Methodological issues** -- Inconsistent N across Table 1 (N=16 for SciERC vs N=8 for others), formula-implementation discrepancy, missing confidence intervals, and only 10 references in the bibliography.\n\n**Strongest elements:** The diagnostic analysis (inverted-U capability pattern, compound error decomposition for RE, temperature as capability filter) is genuinely insightful and represents the paper's best path to acceptance. The +3.9 F1 practical gain and cross-dataset/cross-model replication are solid.\n\n**Path to acceptance:** Add stronger baselines (especially log-prob and USC), fix the formula discrepancy, standardize experimental conditions in Table 1, expand references to 30+, and reframe as \"set-aware consistency scoring with diagnostic analysis.\"\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":181987,"totalTokens":103738,"totalToolUseCount":29,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4093,"cache_read_input_tokens":99145,"output_tokens":499,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4093},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":499,"cache_read_input_tokens":99145,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4093,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4093,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
