{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PqGVUgGRLBk2TywjwnAcmG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T093336\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: borderline reject (3/6)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T10:17:42.798575+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Reviewer R1: Methodology Soundness & Experimental Rigor\n9\t\n10\t## Summary\n11\t\n12\tThe paper proposes structural self-consistency (soft Jaccard coefficient, SJ) as a confidence signal for information extraction test-time scaling, comparing it against Fleiss' Kappa (FK) via Spearman rank correlation with instance-level F1. Experiments span 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, and 2 model families. The core finding is that SJ outperforms FK for NER (rho=0.41 vs 0.29 on SciERC) but fails on relation extraction (rho=0.26 on full set). The paper frames the RE failure diagnostically, identifying compound error propagation as the cause and documenting an inverted-U pattern across difficulty strata.\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Strengths\n17\t\n18\t**S1. Well-motivated problem formulation.** Extending self-consistency from atomic reasoning outputs to structured prediction is a natural and timely question. The paper correctly identifies that surface agreement metrics (FK) miss structural similarity, and the soft Jaccard formulation is a principled response to this gap.\n19\t\n20\t**S2. Honest treatment of negative results.** The paper does not hide the RE failure or the OntoNotes weakness. The framing as \"when does it work\" rather than \"it always works\" is appropriate and scientifically mature. The progressive narrowing of claims through the paper (SciERC strong → CoNLL moderate → OntoNotes weak → RE fails) is intellectually honest.\n21\t\n22\t**S3. Comprehensive experimental coverage for an empirical study.** 11 experiments covering N-scaling, cross-dataset, cross-model, temperature sensitivity, adaptive compute, capability stratification, selection curves, and OOD robustness. This is thorough for the scope of the paper. All planned experiments are reported.\n23\t\n24\t**S4. The inverted-U finding is genuinely insightful.** The observation that consistency metrics are most informative for medium-difficulty instances — where the model exhibits genuine uncertainty rather than consistent success or failure — provides a principled explanation for why overall rho is moderate and has real diagnostic value.\n25\t\n26\t**S5. Statistical rigor in primary comparisons.** Permutation tests for significance, bootstrap CIs for stratification analysis, and reporting both full and conditional sets are all good practices.\n27\t\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t## Weaknesses\n31\t\n32\t**W1. (Major) The soft Jaccard denominator choice is not well-justified and potentially problematic.** The paper defines SJ(E_i, E_j) = sum_matches / max(|E_i|, |E_j|). This is not the standard Jaccard coefficient, which uses |union| as denominator. Using max(|E_i|, |E_j|) means two sets of size 10 and 3, where all 3 match, get SJ = 3/10 = 0.30, whereas standard Jaccard would give 3/10 = 0.30 as well in this case but diverges in others. More critically, when both sets are empty (model produces nothing), the denominator is 0 — the paper does not discuss this edge case. When one set is empty and the other is not, SJ = 0/|E_j| = 0, which is correct, but the empty-empty case needs explicit handling. The paper should justify why max() rather than |union| or |E_i| + |E_j| - |matched|, and discuss the sensitivity of results to this choice.\n33\t\n34\t**W2. (Major) Fleiss' Kappa is a weak baseline — the paper lacks comparison against stronger alternatives.** FK applied to BIO tags is a reasonable surface-level baseline, but the paper positions SJ as \"the\" structural consistency metric without comparing against obvious alternatives: (a) token-level probability entropy/confidence from the LLM itself, (b) NER-specific voting schemes (e.g., entity-level majority voting where entities appearing in >50% of samples are selected), (c) sequence-level log-probability, (d) other set similarity metrics (e.g., entity-level F1 between sample pairs instead of soft Jaccard). The claim that \"structural consistency outperforms surface agreement\" is only validated against one specific surface metric. Without stronger baselines, the contribution is hard to calibrate.\n35\t\n36\t**W3. (Major) Spearman rho alone is an insufficient evaluation metric for a confidence signal.** A confidence signal's utility depends on more than rank correlation with accuracy: (a) AUROC — can the metric distinguish high-quality from low-quality outputs at a binary threshold? (b) Calibration — is the metric well-calibrated (i.e., instances with SJ=0.8 actually have ~80% chance of being correct)? (c) Kendall's tau — provides robustness check against tied ranks, which are likely abundant since SJ produces many ties when sets are small. (d) Downstream task utility — the adaptive compute experiment partially addresses this, but only on SciERC NER. Reporting only Spearman rho leaves open the possibility that SJ has better rank correlation but worse calibration, which would limit its practical utility.\n37\t\n38\t**W4. (Major) The type-granularity trend is based on 3 data points with confounded variables.** The paper claims that SJ's advantage decreases with entity type granularity (4 types → 6 types → 18 types maps to +7pp → +12pp → +3pp SJ-FK gap). But: (a) this is 3 data points — not enough to establish any trend; (b) the mapping is not monotonic (CoNLL with 4 types has a smaller gap than SciERC with 6 types); (c) the three datasets differ in domain, sentence length, entity density, annotation quality, model baseline performance, and test set size — type count is heavily confounded. The paper acknowledges this (\"suggestive rather than conclusive\") but then uses this trend as a central organizing principle for the narrative, which overstates the evidence.\n39\t\n40\t**W5. (Moderate) The conditional set definition is ad hoc and the paper does not analyze sensitivity to the threshold.** The conditional set excludes instances where all N samples produce F1=0. This is a binary heuristic — an instance with one sample at F1=0.01 and seven at F1=0.00 is included while one with all eight at F1=0.00 is excluded, despite being nearly identical in difficulty. The paper acknowledges \"the boundary is fuzzy\" but does not report how conditional rho varies as the threshold changes (e.g., exclude instances where >75% of samples are F1=0, or where max sample F1 < 0.1). This sensitivity analysis is critical for the RE conditional claim (rho=0.33), which is the paper's main defense of SJ on RE.\n41\t\n42\t**W6. (Moderate) Only 2 models in the 8B parameter range — generalizability to larger models and API-based models is entirely untested.** Both Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B are open-weight 8B models fine-tuned with LoRA. The SJ-FK gap narrows from +12pp (Qwen) to +7pp (LLaMA), raising the question of whether the gap would vanish or invert for stronger models where the model is already well-calibrated and surface agreement may suffice. No experiments with 70B+ models or API-based models (GPT-4, Claude) are included.\n43\t\n44\t**W7. (Moderate) The adaptive compute gain (+1.8pp, 14.8% of oracle headroom) is underwhelming for a TTS paper.** The practical utility of the proposed metric is demonstrated only on SciERC NER, with a +1.8pp gain over greedy and only +0.8pp over FK-based selection. At 16x the inference cost (N=16 samples), this is a poor cost-benefit ratio. No adaptive compute results are reported for the other datasets or RE, leaving open whether SJ-based selection helps beyond SciERC.\n45\t\n46\t**W8. (Minor) Figure 11 is \"described textually due to space constraints\" — this is a missing figure.** Selection curves are a key visualization for TTS papers. Replacing the figure with a table (Table 11) is acceptable but suboptimal; a line plot would more clearly show the diminishing returns pattern and the divergence between oracle and selection methods.\n47\t\n48\t**W9. (Minor) The bipartite matching in soft Jaccard has O(n^3) complexity but the paper does not discuss computational overhead.** For instances with many entities, the Hungarian algorithm for optimal matching adds non-trivial cost on top of the N(N-1)/2 pairwise comparisons. The paper should report wall-clock time for SJ computation relative to FK and relative to the inference cost itself.\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## Questions for Authors\n53\t\n54\t**Q1.** How does SJ behave when both sample outputs are empty (zero entities)? Is SJ defined as 1.0 (perfect agreement on \"nothing\") or 0.0? This edge case matters significantly for RE where 42-51% of samples have F1=0.\n55\t\n56\t**Q2.** Have you tried entity-level majority voting (select entities appearing in >K/N samples) as an alternative to SJ-based whole-output selection? This is a natural structured consistency approach that the paper does not consider.\n57\t\n58\t**Q3.** For the conditional set analysis: how does conditional rho change if you use a softer threshold (e.g., exclude instances where max sample F1 < 0.05, or < 0.10, or where >90% of samples are F1=0)?\n59\t\n60\t**Q4.** The SJ-FK gap on CoNLL-2003 (+7pp) is smaller than on SciERC (+12pp) despite CoNLL having fewer entity types (4 vs 6). This contradicts the type-granularity narrative. What is your explanation?\n61\t\n62\t**Q5.** Have you computed token-level probability entropy or mean sequence log-probability as a baseline? These are standard confidence estimation methods for LLMs and would be much cheaper to compute than N-sample consistency.\n63\t\n64\t**Q6.** The paper reports rho for Qwen3-8B at N=8 as 0.383 (Table 1, N=8 column) and also mentions this is from pilot_004 at T=1.0. But the main NER results claim T=0.7 as default. Are the N=8 and N=16 columns in Table 1 at the same temperature? If not, the N-scaling comparison is confounded by temperature.\n65\t\n66\t**Q7.** Why is adaptive compute evaluated only on SciERC NER? The paper's strongest practical claim would be strengthened by showing SJ-based selection on CoNLL-2003 and OntoNotes.\n67\t\n68\t---\n69\t\n70\t## Missing Experiments/Comparisons\n71\t\n72\t1. **Token-probability baselines.** Mean sequence log-probability, token-level entropy, or P(y|x) as confidence signals — these are cheap, well-understood alternatives that must be compared against to justify the cost of multi-sample consistency.\n73\t\n74\t2. **Entity-level majority voting.** A natural structured consistency baseline that uses the same N samples but selects per-entity rather than per-output.\n75\t\n76\t3. **Threshold sensitivity for conditional set.** Varying the all-zero cutoff to show robustness (or fragility) of the conditional rho claim.\n77\t\n78\t4. **Kendall's tau and/or AUROC.** Alternative evaluation metrics for the confidence signal.\n79\t\n80\t5. **Adaptive compute on all datasets.** SJ-based selection results on CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes, and RE — not just SciERC NER.\n81\t\n82\t6. **Larger models (70B+ or API-based).** At least one experiment showing whether the SJ advantage holds for models with higher baseline performance.\n83\t\n84\t7. **Ablation on soft Jaccard design choices.** max() vs union denominator, with vs without type matching, hard vs soft span matching — to understand which component of SJ drives the advantage over FK.\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## Methodology Score: 3/6\n89\t\n90\tThe soft Jaccard formulation is reasonable but has unjustified design choices (denominator) and untested edge cases. The evaluation framework (Spearman rho only, one weak baseline) is insufficient for a paper about confidence estimation. The type-granularity trend — positioned as a central finding — rests on 3 confounded data points. The conditional set analysis, which is critical for the RE diagnostic claim, lacks threshold sensitivity analysis.\n91\t\n92\t## Preliminary Overall Score: 3/6 (Borderline Reject)\n93\t\n94\t**Justification:** The paper addresses a timely question (TTS for structured prediction) and demonstrates scientific honesty in reporting negative/weak results. However, for a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate, the contributions are insufficient:\n95\t\n96\t- The *methodological contribution* (soft Jaccard) is a straightforward adaptation of set similarity to structured outputs — well-executed but not novel.\n97\t- The *empirical contribution* is thorough in breadth (11 experiments) but shallow in depth — the evaluation uses only Spearman rho against a single weak baseline, leaving the practical utility of SJ as a confidence signal poorly characterized.\n98\t- The *analytical contribution* (inverted-U pattern, type-granularity trend, compound error propagation) is the strongest aspect, but the type-granularity claim is unsupported by the data (3 confounded points, non-monotonic), and the inverted-U pattern, while insightful, uses oracle F1 strata that are unavailable at test time, limiting practical applicability.\n99\t- The *practical impact* is minimal: +1.8pp on one dataset at 16x inference cost, with 85% of oracle headroom uncaptured.\n100\t\n101\tThe paper would benefit from: (1) stronger baselines including probability-based confidence, (2) evaluation metrics beyond Spearman rho, (3) more rigorous analysis of the type-granularity claim or dropping it as a central narrative, and (4) demonstration of practical utility across all evaluated datasets.\n102\t\n103\tEMNLP Findings may be realistic if the above concerns are partially addressed; Main conference acceptance would require substantial additional evidence and stronger baselines."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"47bca196-4c67-4d68-8d31-da0cb691f636","uuid":"8d17868c-1110-4833-94b5-6bfb2b9912bb","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:19:11.552Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T093336/median/review_report.md","content":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260514T093336\ntier: median\noverall_score: borderline reject (3/6)\ntimestamp: 2026-05-14T10:17:42.798575+00:00\n---\n\n# Reviewer R1: Methodology Soundness & Experimental Rigor\n\n## Summary\n\nThe paper proposes structural self-consistency (soft Jaccard coefficient, SJ) as a confidence signal for information extraction test-time scaling, comparing it against Fleiss' Kappa (FK) via Spearman rank correlation with instance-level F1. Experiments span 3 NER datasets, 1 RE dataset, and 2 model families. The core finding is that SJ outperforms FK for NER (rho=0.41 vs 0.29 on SciERC) but fails on relation extraction (rho=0.26 on full set). The paper frames the RE failure diagnostically, identifying compound error propagation as the cause and documenting an inverted-U pattern across difficulty strata.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-motivated problem formulation.** Extending self-consistency from atomic reasoning outputs to structured prediction is a natural and timely question. The paper correctly identifies that surface agreement metrics (FK) miss structural similarity, and the soft Jaccard formulation is a principled response to this gap.\n\n**S2. Honest treatment of negative results.** The paper does not hide the RE failure or the OntoNotes weakness. The framing as \"when does it work\" rather than \"it always works\" is appropriate and scientifically mature. The progressive narrowing of claims through the paper (SciERC strong → CoNLL moderate → OntoNotes weak → RE fails) is intellectually honest.\n\n**S3. Comprehensive experimental coverage for an empirical study.** 11 experiments covering N-scaling, cross-dataset, cross-model, temperature sensitivity, adaptive compute, capability stratification, selection curves, and OOD robustness. This is thorough for the scope of the paper. All planned experiments are reported.\n\n**S4. The inverted-U finding is genuinely insightful.** The observation that consistency metrics are most informative for medium-difficulty instances — where the model exhibits genuine uncertainty rather than consistent success or failure — provides a principled explanation for why overall rho is moderate and has real diagnostic value.\n\n**S5. Statistical rigor in primary comparisons.** Permutation tests for significance, bootstrap CIs for stratification analysis, and reporting both full and conditional sets are all good practices.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Major) The soft Jaccard denominator choice is not well-justified and potentially problematic.** The paper defines SJ(E_i, E_j) = sum_matches / max(|E_i|, |E_j|). This is not the standard Jaccard coefficient, which uses |union| as denominator. Using max(|E_i|, |E_j|) means two sets of size 10 and 3, where all 3 match, get SJ = 3/10 = 0.30, whereas standard Jaccard would give 3/10 = 0.30 as well in this case but diverges in others. More critically, when both sets are empty (model produces nothing), the denominator is 0 — the paper does not discuss this edge case. When one set is empty and the other is not, SJ = 0/|E_j| = 0, which is correct, but the empty-empty case needs explicit handling. The paper should justify why max() rather than |union| or |E_i| + |E_j| - |matched|, and discuss the sensitivity of results to this choice.\n\n**W2. (Major) Fleiss' Kappa is a weak baseline — the paper lacks comparison against stronger alternatives.** FK applied to BIO tags is a reasonable surface-level baseline, but the paper positions SJ as \"the\" structural consistency metric without comparing against obvious alternatives: (a) token-level probability entropy/confidence from the LLM itself, (b) NER-specific voting schemes (e.g., entity-level majority voting where entities appearing in >50% of samples are selected), (c) sequence-level log-probability, (d) other set similarity metrics (e.g., entity-level F1 between sample pairs instead of soft Jaccard). The claim that \"structural consistency outperforms surface agreement\" is only validated against one specific surface metric. Without stronger baselines, the contribution is hard to calibrate.\n\n**W3. (Major) Spearman rho alone is an insufficient evaluation metric for a confidence signal.** A confidence signal's utility depends on more than rank correlation with accuracy: (a) AUROC — can the metric distinguish high-quality from low-quality outputs at a binary threshold? (b) Calibration — is the metric well-calibrated (i.e., instances with SJ=0.8 actually have ~80% chance of being correct)? (c) Kendall's tau — provides robustness check against tied ranks, which are likely abundant since SJ produces many ties when sets are small. (d) Downstream task utility — the adaptive compute experiment partially addresses this, but only on SciERC NER. Reporting only Spearman rho leaves open the possibility that SJ has better rank correlation but worse calibration, which would limit its practical utility.\n\n**W4. (Major) The type-granularity trend is based on 3 data points with confounded variables.** The paper claims that SJ's advantage decreases with entity type granularity (4 types → 6 types → 18 types maps to +7pp → +12pp → +3pp SJ-FK gap). But: (a) this is 3 data points — not enough to establish any trend; (b) the mapping is not monotonic (CoNLL with 4 types has a smaller gap than SciERC with 6 types); (c) the three datasets differ in domain, sentence length, entity density, annotation quality, model baseline performance, and test set size — type count is heavily confounded. The paper acknowledges this (\"suggestive rather than conclusive\") but then uses this trend as a central organizing principle for the narrative, which overstates the evidence.\n\n**W5. (Moderate) The conditional set definition is ad hoc and the paper does not analyze sensitivity to the threshold.** The conditional set excludes instances where all N samples produce F1=0. This is a binary heuristic — an instance with one sample at F1=0.01 and seven at F1=0.00 is included while one with all eight at F1=0.00 is excluded, despite being nearly identical in difficulty. The paper acknowledges \"the boundary is fuzzy\" but does not report how conditional rho varies as the threshold changes (e.g., exclude instances where >75% of samples are F1=0, or where max sample F1 < 0.1). This sensitivity analysis is critical for the RE conditional claim (rho=0.33), which is the paper's main defense of SJ on RE.\n\n**W6. (Moderate) Only 2 models in the 8B parameter range — generalizability to larger models and API-based models is entirely untested.** Both Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B are open-weight 8B models fine-tuned with LoRA. The SJ-FK gap narrows from +12pp (Qwen) to +7pp (LLaMA), raising the question of whether the gap would vanish or invert for stronger models where the model is already well-calibrated and surface agreement may suffice. No experiments with 70B+ models or API-based models (GPT-4, Claude) are included.\n\n**W7. (Moderate) The adaptive compute gain (+1.8pp, 14.8% of oracle headroom) is underwhelming for a TTS paper.** The practical utility of the proposed metric is demonstrated only on SciERC NER, with a +1.8pp gain over greedy and only +0.8pp over FK-based selection. At 16x the inference cost (N=16 samples), this is a poor cost-benefit ratio. No adaptive compute results are reported for the other datasets or RE, leaving open whether SJ-based selection helps beyond SciERC.\n\n**W8. (Minor) Figure 11 is \"described textually due to space constraints\" — this is a missing figure.** Selection curves are a key visualization for TTS papers. Replacing the figure with a table (Table 11) is acceptable but suboptimal; a line plot would more clearly show the diminishing returns pattern and the divergence between oracle and selection methods.\n\n**W9. (Minor) The bipartite matching in soft Jaccard has O(n^3) complexity but the paper does not discuss computational overhead.** For instances with many entities, the Hungarian algorithm for optimal matching adds non-trivial cost on top of the N(N-1)/2 pairwise comparisons. The paper should report wall-clock time for SJ computation relative to FK and relative to the inference cost itself.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** How does SJ behave when both sample outputs are empty (zero entities)? Is SJ defined as 1.0 (perfect agreement on \"nothing\") or 0.0? This edge case matters significantly for RE where 42-51% of samples have F1=0.\n\n**Q2.** Have you tried entity-level majority voting (select entities appearing in >K/N samples) as an alternative to SJ-based whole-output selection? This is a natural structured consistency approach that the paper does not consider.\n\n**Q3.** For the conditional set analysis: how does conditional rho change if you use a softer threshold (e.g., exclude instances where max sample F1 < 0.05, or < 0.10, or where >90% of samples are F1=0)?\n\n**Q4.** The SJ-FK gap on CoNLL-2003 (+7pp) is smaller than on SciERC (+12pp) despite CoNLL having fewer entity types (4 vs 6). This contradicts the type-granularity narrative. What is your explanation?\n\n**Q5.** Have you computed token-level probability entropy or mean sequence log-probability as a baseline? These are standard confidence estimation methods for LLMs and would be much cheaper to compute than N-sample consistency.\n\n**Q6.** The paper reports rho for Qwen3-8B at N=8 as 0.383 (Table 1, N=8 column) and also mentions this is from pilot_004 at T=1.0. But the main NER results claim T=0.7 as default. Are the N=8 and N=16 columns in Table 1 at the same temperature? If not, the N-scaling comparison is confounded by temperature.\n\n**Q7.** Why is adaptive compute evaluated only on SciERC NER? The paper's strongest practical claim would be strengthened by showing SJ-based selection on CoNLL-2003 and OntoNotes.\n\n---\n\n## Missing Experiments/Comparisons\n\n1. **Token-probability baselines.** Mean sequence log-probability, token-level entropy, or P(y|x) as confidence signals — these are cheap, well-understood alternatives that must be compared against to justify the cost of multi-sample consistency.\n\n2. **Entity-level majority voting.** A natural structured consistency baseline that uses the same N samples but selects per-entity rather than per-output.\n\n3. **Threshold sensitivity for conditional set.** Varying the all-zero cutoff to show robustness (or fragility) of the conditional rho claim.\n\n4. **Kendall's tau and/or AUROC.** Alternative evaluation metrics for the confidence signal.\n\n5. **Adaptive compute on all datasets.** SJ-based selection results on CoNLL-2003, OntoNotes, and RE — not just SciERC NER.\n\n6. **Larger models (70B+ or API-based).** At least one experiment showing whether the SJ advantage holds for models with higher baseline performance.\n\n7. **Ablation on soft Jaccard design choices.** max() vs union denominator, with vs without type matching, hard vs soft span matching — to understand which component of SJ drives the advantage over FK.\n\n---\n\n## Methodology Score: 3/6\n\nThe soft Jaccard formulation is reasonable but has unjustified design choices (denominator) and untested edge cases. The evaluation framework (Spearman rho only, one weak baseline) is insufficient for a paper about confidence estimation. The type-granularity trend — positioned as a central finding — rests on 3 confounded data points. The conditional set analysis, which is critical for the RE diagnostic claim, lacks threshold sensitivity analysis.\n\n## Preliminary Overall Score: 3/6 (Borderline Reject)\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a timely question (TTS for structured prediction) and demonstrates scientific honesty in reporting negative/weak results. However, for a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate, the contributions are insufficient:\n\n- The *methodological contribution* (soft Jaccard) is a straightforward adaptation of set similarity to structured outputs — well-executed but not novel.\n- The *empirical contribution* is thorough in breadth (11 experiments) but shallow in depth — the evaluation uses only Spearman rho against a single weak baseline, leaving the practical utility of SJ as a confidence signal poorly characterized.\n- The *analytical contribution* (inverted-U pattern, type-granularity trend, compound error propagation) is the strongest aspect, but the type-granularity claim is unsupported by the data (3 confounded points, non-monotonic), and the inverted-U pattern, while insightful, uses oracle F1 strata that are unavailable at test time, limiting practical applicability.\n- The *practical impact* is minimal: +1.8pp on one dataset at 16x inference cost, with 85% of oracle headroom uncaptured.\n\nThe paper would benefit from: (1) stronger baselines including probability-based confidence, (2) evaluation metrics beyond Spearman rho, (3) more rigorous analysis of the type-granularity claim or dropping it as a central narrative, and (4) demonstration of practical utility across all evaluated datasets.\n\nEMNLP Findings may be realistic if the above concerns are partially addressed; Main conference acceptance would require substantial additional evidence and stronger baselines.","numLines":103,"startLine":1,"totalLines":103}}}
